msgid ""
"Quarkus Insights #253: What the Build Analytics Tell Us About the Quarkus "
"Community"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This summary was generated using AI, reviewed by humans - https://youtube."
"com/live/VXly6egPSTs[watch the video] for the full story."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Episode 253 of Quarkus Insights took a different angle from the usual "
"technical deep-dives. Bruno Baptista and Max Rydahl Andersen presented a "
"year-over-year analysis of the opt-in build analytics data that Quarkus has "
"been collecting from the community, comparing May 2025 to May 2026. The "
"result was a snapshot of how the Quarkus developer community is evolving — "
"which OS developers use, how fast they are adopting new Java versions, which "
"extensions are growing fastest, and where in the world Quarkus is being "
"built."
msgstr ""

msgid "What the Build Analytics Are (and Are Not)"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Before the numbers, Bruno explained the nature of the data. Quarkus build "
"analytics are **opt-in only**. The first time a developer uses https://"
"quarkus.io/guides/dev-mode-differences[dev mode], they are prompted to "
"contribute anonymous build-time data. If they agree, each subsequent build "
"or dev-mode execution sends a small JSON payload."
msgstr ""

msgid "What is collected:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Build type (dev mode vs. regular build)"
msgstr ""

msgid "Extensions used"
msgstr ""

msgid "Java version"
msgstr ""

msgid "Operating system"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Build tool (https://maven.apache.org/[Maven] or https://gradle.org/[Gradle])"
msgstr ""

msgid "Time zone (used as a geographic proxy)"
msgstr ""

msgid "Whether https://www.graalvm.org/[GraalVM] native compilation was used"
msgstr ""

msgid "Quarkus version"
msgstr ""

msgid "JVM language (Java, https://kotlinlang.org/[Kotlin], etc.)"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"What is explicitly **not** collected: source code, IP addresses, or any "
"other personal data."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The analytics file sent is stored locally at `target/quarkus-build-analytics."
"json`, so developers can inspect exactly what is being shared. To check or "
"change the setting, the local config lives at `~/.redhat/` on your home "
"directory — a `disabled: false` entry means analytics are on."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The team noted a deliberate gap: only extensions from known group IDs (the "
"core Quarkus and a handful of community namespaces) are tracked. Internal or "
"corporate extensions are never reported, to avoid inadvertently revealing "
"what proprietary components a company is using. Community extensions wanting "
"to be included can contact the team."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Because the data is opt-in, the absolute numbers are never shown publicly — "
"the team looks at relative growth and proportions. Maven download stats (opt-"
"out rather than opt-in) show higher absolute numbers, but the two data "
"sources tend to tell consistent stories about trends."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The analytics page at https://quarkus.io/usage/[quarkus.io/usage] has more "
"detail on what is collected and why."
msgstr ""

msgid "Community Growth"
msgstr ""

msgid "The headline numbers for May 2026 vs May 2025:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Monthly active users up 16%** — existing users are building more, not just "
"that there are more of them"
msgstr ""

msgid "**Total builds up 26%**"
msgstr ""

msgid "**CI/production builds up 9.3%**"
msgstr ""

msgid "**Dev-mode builds flat** (down ~2%, within seasonal variation)"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**New users flat** — growth is coming from deeper engagement by existing "
"users, not a surge of first-time adopters"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Max observed that AI-assisted development likely explains part of the shift: "
"developers using agents to write and run code may run fewer manual start-"
"stop-start dev cycles, but still trigger dependency downloads and CI builds. "
"The build analytics capture the latter but undercount the former."
msgstr ""

msgid "Operating System: Windows Takes the Lead"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The most eye-catching slide: **Windows 11 is now the most-used OS among "
"Quarkus developers**, overtaking macOS which held the top spot last year. "
"Linux slipped slightly."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Bruno attributed this to two overlapping factors: a large migration from "
"Windows 10 to Windows 11, and what looks like growing enterprise adoption — "
"organizations where developers work on locked-down corporate Windows laptops."
" Max noted that in most developer tooling data over the years, Windows has "
"consistently been around 30-40%, macOS around 30%, and Linux the remainder, "
"so seeing Windows at the top is unusual and significant for prioritization "
"decisions."
msgstr ""

msgid "The data reinforces that Windows support is not optional for Quarkus."
msgstr ""

msgid "Geographic Spread"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Time zone is used as a geographic proxy. The top three cities by build "
"activity were São Paulo (Brazil), Germany (multiple cities), and Kolkata "
"(India). Bruno noted that China is growing fast — roughly doubling year-over-"
"year — though time zone data for China is complicated because the country "
"spans multiple natural time zones but uses a single official one, meaning a "
"large number of users are concentrated in a single time zone."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The broader trend is clear: Quarkus is no longer primarily a European "
"project. Latin America, India, and China are all growing, and activity in "
"Bangkok, Mexico City, and Bogotá is notable — Bogotá now has more build "
"activity in the last 30 days than London."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"At the other end, the least-active time zones include small Pacific islands, "
"Malta, San Marino, and — to some amusement on the stream — Perth, Australia."
msgstr ""

msgid "Java Version Adoption"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Java 25, released in September 2025, already accounts for **22% of all "
"Quarkus builds** — a rate faster than any previous Java version adoption. "
"Java 21 and Java 25 together now represent around **76% of all builds**."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This is directly relevant to Quarkus 4, which will require Java 21 or later. "
"The data suggests the community is already well-positioned for that "
"transition. Java 17 adoption has roughly halved since last year and "
"continues to fall."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Max attributed the acceleration to a combination of factors: the painful "
"breaking changes that accompanied older version jumps are largely behind the "
"ecosystem, and Java 25 offers practical, visible benefits (notably "
"performance) that make upgrading feel worthwhile rather than just necessary. "
"The Quarkus team did a significant amount of work across two or three "
"releases to handle Java 25's stricter warnings around native memory access, "
"with extensions now able to declare the JVM flags they need so Quarkus can "
"set them automatically at launch."
msgstr ""

msgid "Build Tool"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Maven remains dominant at **85% of builds**, with Maven 3.9 being the most-"
"used version. Maven 4.x is beginning to appear. Gradle sits at roughly 15%, "
"consistent with what the https://code.quarkus.io/[code.quarkus.io] project-"
"creation data shows — the two data sources agree, which Bruno took as a sign "
"the analytics are representative."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Maven's share actually grew slightly (+2.5 percentage points) year-over-year."
" The team's default wrapper version being Maven 3.9 likely contributes to "
"that number's persistence."
msgstr ""

msgid "GraalVM Native Adoption"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Native compilation via GraalVM dropped from 2.5% to 2% of builds. Bruno "
"interpreted this as developers building less natively on local machines, not "
"a sign that native is less important in production."
msgstr ""

msgid "Extension Trends"
msgstr ""

msgid "Core Extensions: The Boring Ones Win"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The most-used core extensions are exactly what you would expect for "
"production applications: REST, https://hibernate.org/[Hibernate], health, "
"config, security, and https://www.postgresql.org/[PostgreSQL]. The top eight "
"extensions appear in more than 40% of all applications."
msgstr ""

msgid "The standout growth numbers:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Flyway up 72%** — the biggest mover, strongly associated with production "
"workloads. Bruno linked this partly to a licence change by a competing tool "
"(https://www.liquibase.com/[Liquibase]) last year, though Liquibase itself "
"did not appear in the top 20."
msgstr ""

msgid "**JDBC up 42%**"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Quartz scheduler growing fast** — people are using it to schedule tasks, "
"reflecting real production use"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**RESTEasy Classic migration nearly complete** — the classic REST Jackson "
"extension dropped from 19% to 11% while the new Quarkus REST Jackson climbed "
"from 67% to 86%"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Micrometer and https://opentelemetry.io/[OpenTelemetry] both up ~31%**, "
"growing in parallel"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Max's observation: the extensions rising fastest are \"the boring ones\" — "
"the tools you need to actually build and run a production service. The "
"novelty extensions (AI, reactive, event-driven frameworks) appear further "
"down the list and take time to climb."
msgstr ""

msgid "Quarkiverse Extensions: The Top 20"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The most-used Quarkiverse extension is Amazon S3 at 3.8% (measured as a "
"share of all builds, not just Quarkiverse). Other notable entries:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**https://cxf.apache.org/[Apache CXF]** — a significant presence, suggesting "
"legacy SOAP/web services integration workloads"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**https://www.vaultproject.io/[HashiCorp Vault]** — up sharply, consistent "
"with the production-readiness trend; applications need to fetch credentials "
"securely"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**https://poi.apache.org/[Apache POI]** — document generation, particularly "
"Excel and Word files"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"MCP server* — new last year, already visible in the top 20; the extension "
"for building https://modelcontextprotocol.io/[Model Context Protocol] "
"servers did not exist in the prior-year comparison"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"JGit rising — developers using Quarkus to interact with Git repositories "
"programmatically"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**https://tika.apache.org/[Apache Tika]** rising — document content "
"extraction, almost certainly being used in agentic and RAG workloads"
msgstr ""

msgid "AI Extensions: Shifting Landscape"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"On the AI side, LangChain4j dropped 40% in share, while direct OpenAI "
"integration held or grew. Max and Bruno interpreted this as a maturing "
"pattern: less experimentation with high-level orchestration wrappers, more "
"direct API usage and local model inference."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"AWS-related extensions declined: Lambda usage took a 40% dent year-over-year."
" Maven download data tells a different story — Amazon extensions have been "
"growing faster than almost anything else — so Bruno suspected the opt-in "
"analytics are capturing a different slice of the AWS user population than "
"the overall download stats."
msgstr ""

msgid "Apache Camel"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"https://camel.apache.org/[Apache Camel] extensions saw notable declines in "
"share. Raw adoption grew slightly, but slower than the overall platform "
"growth, so the relative share fell."
msgstr ""

msgid "Language"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Java remains dominant at nearly 97% of builds. Kotlin grew almost 12% year-"
"over-year in absolute terms — still a small share, but meaningful enough "
"that the team treats Kotlin investment as informed by real data rather than "
"guesswork."
msgstr ""

msgid "The Typical Quarkus Developer (May 2026)"
msgstr ""

msgid "Based on the data, the modal Quarkus developer:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Uses **Windows 11 or macOS**"
msgstr ""

msgid "Is on **Java 21, migrating to Java 25**"
msgstr ""

msgid "Builds with **Maven 3.9**"
msgstr ""

msgid "Runs **PostgreSQL**"
msgstr ""

msgid "Has a stack of **REST, health checks, and Hibernate/Panache**"
msgstr ""

msgid "Is located in **Latin America or Europe**"
msgstr ""

msgid "What This Data Drives"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Bruno and Max were both clear that the analytics are not vanity metrics. "
"Kotlin adoption data informs how much the team invests in Kotlin support. "
"Gradle's share informs tooling investment. Extension growth rates influence "
"where limited engineering resources get focused. The Java version "
"distribution directly shaped the decision to target Java 21+ for Quarkus 4."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The team encouraged anyone not yet opted in — especially Kotlin or Gradle "
"users — to enable the analytics, since the current numbers likely undercount "
"those populations. More representative data leads to better prioritization "
"decisions."
msgstr ""

msgid "Upcoming"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The episode also mentioned that Quarkus 3.37 has been released, and "
"development on Quarkus 4 continues alongside the 3.x series (expected "
"through 3.40). Quarkus Insights will be on a summer break for two weeks; the "
"next episode will return to domain-driven design and hexagonal architecture."
msgstr ""

msgid "Key Takeaways"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Build analytics are opt-in and anonymous** — only build metadata is "
"collected, never source code or IP addresses."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Monthly active users grew 16%** and total builds grew 26% year-over-year."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Windows 11 is now the most-used OS** among Quarkus developers, overtaking "
"macOS."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Java 25 adoption is at 22%** — faster than any previous Java version "
"uptake; Java 21 + 25 together are 76% of all builds."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Quarkus 4 will require Java 21+**, and the community is already largely "
"ready."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Maven holds at 85%** of builds; Gradle at ~15%."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Production-oriented \"boring\" extensions are growing fastest** — Flyway "
"(+72%), JDBC (+42%), Vault, Quartz."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**RESTEasy Classic migration is nearly complete** — the new REST Jackson "
"extension is at 86% share."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**The community is diversifying geographically** — Latin America, India, and "
"China are all growing strongly."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**MCP server and Tika extensions are rising**, suggesting agentic workloads "
"are moving from experimentation into real applications."
msgstr ""

msgid "Conclusion"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The build analytics give the Quarkus team — and the wider community — a "
"grounded, data-driven view of how Quarkus is being used in practice. The "
"picture that emerges from May 2026 is of a maturing, production-focused "
"ecosystem: developers are building more, upgrading Java versions faster than "
"ever, reaching for production essentials like schema migration and secrets "
"management, and coming from a more geographically diverse set of time zones "
"than ever before."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If you want to help make the data more representative, you can enable build "
"analytics by setting `disabled: false` in `~/.redhat/` — or just answer "
"\"yes\" next time dev mode prompts you. Every build counts."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Watch the full episode on the <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/"
"quarkusio\">Quarkus YouTube channel</a>. Learn more about what is collected "
"at <a href=\"https://quarkus.io/usage/\">quarkus.io/usage</a> and explore "
"extensions sorted by popularity at <a href=\"https://quarkus.io/extensions/"
"\">quarkus.io/extensions</a>."
msgstr ""
